# Copy to `.env` (which is gitignored) and fill in.
#
# The account must hold cACU on Acurast Canary. Free from https://faucet.acurast.com
# The CLI loads this via dotenv automatically.

ACURAST_MNEMONIC="word word word ... twelve or twenty-four words"

# Optional. If set, the deployment POSTs progress breadcrumbs here. With a quick
# tunnel this is also the only way the per-run hostname gets back to us. Leave
# unset to rely on `acurast devtools <id>` for stdout instead.
WEBHOOK_URL=""

# --- stable hostname (optional, but this is what stops the URL churning) -----
#
# Without these, `start.sh` falls back to an unauthenticated Cloudflare quick
# tunnel: no account, no DNS, but a random hostname minted at boot and dead when
# the job ends. Every restart, and every reassignment to another phone, means
# reading a new URL out of the webhook and pasting it into the app.
#
# With them, the phone attaches as a connector to a *named* tunnel you own. The
# hostname lives in Cloudflare, not in the job, so it is the same URL across
# restarts and across processors — the phone joins the tunnel rather than
# minting its own.
#
# Setup, once:
#   1. Zero Trust -> Networks -> Tunnels -> Create tunnel (type: Cloudflared).
#      Dedicate this tunnel to the verifier; do not share it with another
#      service (see the single-connector note below).
#   2. Copy the string after `--token` in the connector command it shows you.
#      That is CF_TUNNEL_TOKEN. It is base64 JSON {"a":account,"t":tunnel,"s":secret}
#      — a credential; it is delivered to the processor encrypted, keep it out
#      of git and out of logs.
#   3. Tunnel -> Public Hostname -> add `verifier.<your-domain>` -> HTTP ->
#      `localhost:3000`. (PORT in start.sh defaults to 3000.)
#   4. Verify the DNS record actually exists: zone -> DNS -> CNAME `verifier`
#      -> `<tunnel-id>.cfargotunnel.com`, proxied. The dashboard creates it
#      automatically but fails silently if any record for that name already
#      exists, which shows up later as NXDOMAIN with everything else healthy.
#      Recover the tunnel UUID from the token with `base64 -d`.
#
# SINGLE CONNECTOR, DELIBERATELY. Cloudflare treats every connector on a tunnel
# as an interchangeable replica and round-robins between them. That is fine for
# a stateless site and wrong here: each deployment mints its own secp256k1 key,
# so a second phone on this tunnel would sign some attestations with a key the
# client never pinned, and the contract would reject them. `numberOfReplicas` in
# acurast.json stays at 1 for this reason, and start.sh additionally asks the
# public hostname for its /identity at boot and withdraws if the answer is not
# its own key. To run more than one phone, give each its own tunnel and hostname.
CF_TUNNEL_TOKEN=""

# The hostname you configured in step 3, without the scheme — e.g.
# `verifier.example.com`. Only used for reporting and for the single-connector
# check; the routing itself is entirely Cloudflare-side.
VERIFIER_HOSTNAME=""

# --- before you spend a deploy ----------------------------------------------
#
# `local-test/run.sh --smoke` runs the whole entrypoint against a local stand-in
# for the processor and exits 0 on reaching the serve loop. None of the values
# above are needed for it: it supplies its own sink, its own mock bridge, and a
# stub cloudflared, so no real tunnel is opened and no credential is used.
#
# Worth doing every time. It takes ~40 seconds; a phone takes ~2m20s just to
# post its first breadcrumb, and a deployment that fails after that has already
# cost you the fee and the scheduling delay.
#
# What it will NOT catch, because Docker is not proot-on-Android: the netlink
# ban, blocked /proc/net, the network namespace shared between jobs, and the
# real secure element (a mock bridge stands in, so signatures are fake). A green
# smoke run means the shell logic is sound, not that the job will run.
